          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I am 52 and my LDL cholesterol is 165 mg/dL. Does that number alone decide treatment, what risk bands does guidance use, and which modifiable coronary-risk factors does the corpus describe?",
          "answer": "**Does LDL alone decide treatment?** No. The lipid guidance says the decision is driven by estimated 10-year atherosclerotic cardiovascular risk, not by the LDL number alone (*Lipids: screening intervals, risk stratification and statin initiation*).\n\n**What risk bands does the guidance use?** The passages I retrieved do not give the risk bands or numeric thresholds.\n\n**Which modifiable risk factors does the corpus describe?** The *Coronary artery disease* reference lists these contributors:\n- Smoking\n- High blood pressure\n- Raised LDL cholesterol\n- Diabetes\n- Obesity\n- Physical inactivity\n- A diet high in saturated fat and low in vegetables and fiber\n- Chronic kidney disease\n- Inflammatory conditions\n\nThe same passage says age and male sex cannot be changed. It also groups family history of premature coronary disease with the modifiable factors, which seems wrong, since you can't change your family history. Treat that grouping with caution and ask a clinician about your family history.\n\nThe corpus also describes non-drug measures for blood pressure (less salt, a diet rich in vegetables, fruit and whole grains, regular aerobic activity, weight reduction, limiting alcohol, stopping smoking) in the *Hypertension* guidance. Those are framed around blood pressure, not cholesterol.\n\n**What the results did not cover:** the risk-band thresholds, how LDL feeds into the 10-year estimate, and what a 52-year-old with your numbers would be advised to do. A clinician who knows your full history can answer those.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
